    "id": "worst-intensity",
    "question": "In 2023, which property types had the highest median GHG emissions intensity per square foot? Only consider types with at least 20 buildings.",
    "answer": "In 2023, here are the property types with the highest median GHG emissions intensity per square foot (ranked from highest to lowest), considering only types with at least 20 buildings:\n\n| Property Type | Buildings | Median GHG Emissions (kg CO\u2082e/sf) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Supermarket/Grocery Store** | 35 | **5.18** |\n| **Laboratory** | 32 | **4.95** |\n| **Other - Recreation** | 22 | **3.62** |\n| Hotel | 78 | 1.75 |\n| Senior Living Community | 40 | 1.51 |\n| K-12 School | 145 | 1.21 |\n\nThe **top three highest-emitting property types** are:\n\n1. **Supermarket/Grocery Store** at 5.18 kg CO\u2082e/sf (35 buildings)\n2. **Laboratory** at 4.95 kg CO\u2082e/sf (32 buildings)\n3. **Other - Recreation** at 3.62 kg CO\u2082e/sf (22 buildings)\n\nThese types are significantly more carbon-intensive than other major property types. For context, the largest category by count\u2014Multifamily Housing (1,881 buildings)\u2014has a median intensity of just 0.40 kg CO\u2082e/sf, nearly 13\u00d7 lower than supermarkets. This difference is likely driven by supermarkets' high refrigeration loads and 24/7 operations, and laboratories' specialized HVAC and equipment demands.",
    "sql": [
      "\nSELECT b.primary_property_type,\n       COUNT(*) AS n,\n       median(r.ghg_emissions_intensity) AS median_kgco2e_sf\nFROM energy_records r\nJOIN buildings b USING (ose_building_id)\nWHERE r.data_year = 2023 AND r.ghg_emissions_intensity IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY 1\nHAVING n >= 20\nORDER BY median_kgco2e_sf DESC;\n"
    ],
