    "answer": "## Summary\n\nOffice buildings in Seattle have **significantly reduced their weather-normalized energy use intensity (site EUI)** from 2015 through 2025, with notable acceleration during the pandemic and post-pandemic years.\n\n**Key findings:**\n\n- **2015 baseline**: 62.6 kBtu/sf\n- **2019 (pre-pandemic)**: 57.2 kBtu/sf \u2014 a ~8.6% decline over 4 years\n- **2020\u20132021 (pandemic)**: Sharp drop to ~50 kBtu/sf \u2014 a ~12% improvement year-over-year\n- **2024**: 46.6 kBtu/sf\n- **2025**: 44.8 kBtu/sf \u2014 the lowest on record\n\n**Overall improvement**: A ~29% reduction from 2015 to 2025.\n\nThe sharp dip in 2020\u20132021 likely reflects reduced occupancy and operational changes during COVID-19 lockdowns. The continued decline through 2024\u20132025 suggests sustained efficiency improvements, whether from hybrid work adoption, building retrofits, or operational changes.\n\nThe chart has been saved and visualizes this downward trend clearly.",
