    "answer": "## Summary\n\nThe chart shows the trend in the number of buildings reporting energy data to Seattle's benchmarking program from 2015 to 2025.\n\n**Key findings:**\n\n- **2015**: 3,068 buildings reported (the baseline year)\n- **Growth phase (2015\u20132020)**: Steady growth from 3,068 to 3,432 buildings, an increase of ~12%\n- **2021 dip**: A notable drop to 3,137 buildings (likely related to pandemic impacts on reporting)\n- **Recovery & peak (2022\u20132023)**: Strong recovery to 3,431 and then 3,565 buildings (the highest point)\n- **2024\u20132025**: Slight decline to 3,529 and 3,332 buildings\n\nOverall, the program has expanded significantly since 2015, with the current reporting base roughly 8% larger than at the start. The 2021 dip was a temporary disruption, followed by recovery and growth through 2023. The recent slight decline in 2024\u20132025 may reflect demolitions or buildings becoming exempt from the program.\n\nThe data shows strong compliance with Seattle's SMC 22.920 benchmarking requirements, with most of the ~3,700 buildings in the covered universe actively reporting.",
